#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079315} :activity/id #uuid "67f9618d-2121-5f21-8ff1-2bbb8d89a5c7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048498 :topic/id #uuid "a4b9d7e7-30a6-550b-9a00-c1803847c09c" :topic/math-academy-id 613} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079315 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079718 :tutorial/content "The **second fundamental theorem of calculus** (abbreviated FTC II) states the following:\n\n> *If a function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ with $x\\in(a,b),$ and the accumulation function $F(x)$ is defined as*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> F(x) = \\int_a^x f(t)\\,\\textrm d t,\n> $$\n> \n> \n> *then the derivative of $F(x)$ is given by*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> F'(x) = \\dfrac {\\textrm d} {\\textrm dx} \\int_a^x  f(t) \\,\\textrm d t = f(x).\n> $$\n\nLoosely speaking, taking the derivative \"cancels out\" the integral and leaves us with the integrand. This happens because the derivative and the integral are *opposite* operations.\n\nTo demonstrate how this works, let's define the accumulation function $F(x)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\int^{x}_{2} \\sin(t^2)\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nSuppose that we want to differentiate this function to find $F'(x).$ Let's start by differentiating both sides with respect to $x,$ which gives \n\n$$\nF'(x) =  \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\int^{x}_{2} \\sin(t^2)\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the right-hand side is difficult. However, we can apply FTC II and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}({x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's it! This theorem saves us a ton of work and is especially useful when the integral is hard to calculate.\n\n**Note:** To gain further intuition for FTC II, it helps to work out an example in a simple case. Let's define the accumulation function $F(x)$ as an integral that we can actually compute:\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\int^{x}_{2} t^2 \\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nComputing the integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & ={\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}|}_{2}^{x}=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{8}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{8}{3})={x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich matches up with the original integrand. Taking the integral turned $t^2$ into $\\dfrac{x^3}{3}$ minus a constant, and taking the derivative \"reversed\" the process to give us just $x^2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "74916d96-0a48-5708-ae4e-0cce1e62d532" :tutorial/math-academy-id 814 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1264f3b9-3675-53b2-b098-5abbdc342525" :step/math-academy-id 1562 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079316}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079316 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078438 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078312 :question/id #uuid "25a78c7c-e5c0-5048-b12c-6ed4848f5f01" :question/math-academy-id "e-438"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf94b5cf-6407-5931-92df-9f87818e8a71" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050057}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403829}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus"} :step/id #uuid "7d22f17a-59e4-5bba-afd6-c10bb6724990" :step/math-academy-id 1563 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079317}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079317 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078439 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078313 :question/id #uuid "b5e8576b-cd5c-5153-a492-1e8367456744" :question/math-academy-id "e-439"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5075a64-0f91-58cf-b01b-5b71035f0680" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048563}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297471}